BT /F1 11 Tf 14 TL 60 760 Td
(The Gram stain) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (THE GRAM STAIN \(sample notes\)) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Steps) Tj
T* (1. Crystal violet \(primary stain\) is applied to all cells.) Tj
T* (2. Gram's iodine is applied as a mordant, forming a crystal violet-iodine complex.) Tj
T* (3. Decolouriser \(ethanol or acetone\) is applied briefly.) Tj
T* (4. Safranin \(counterstain\) is applied last.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Result) Tj
T* (- Gram-positive cells retain the crystal violet complex \(thick peptidoglycan traps it\) and) Tj
T* (appear purple.) Tj
T* (- Gram-negative cells lose the complex in the decolouriser \(the outer membrane is disrupted) Tj
T* (and the thin wall cannot retain it\) and take up safranin, appearing pink or red.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Common mistakes) Tj
T* (- Over-decolourising a Gram-positive culture can make it falsely appear Gram-negative.) Tj
T* (- Old cultures can stain Gram-variable.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Study tip: remember "purple = peptidoglycan thick, pink = outer membrane".) Tj
ET
endstream
